WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.035 --> 00:00:04.950
I'm June Sarpong and I'm
the author of Diversify.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.470
I was disabled for
four years of my life.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.510
When I was a teenager,
I was hit by a car,

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:11.610
didn't walk for two
years, and then I

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:14.460
had to wear a neck
brace for another two.

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:20.860
The thing that was just
so strange about it

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:24.520
for me was the way
people reacted towards me

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.380
after the accident.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.180
The way people
treated me, it was...

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:30.940
I almost saw a kind
of dumbing down.

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:32.920
It was the most bizarre thing.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:34.660
And I think that
experience is what

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.030
has made me so passionate
about this issue

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:39.420
because I wasn't any
different as a person,

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:42.962
but the world certainly
reacted to me in that way.

00:00:42.962 --> 00:00:44.960
This is a quote
from George Bush,

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.340
not somebody I quote often.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:49.970
And this quote was in relation
to African-American kids

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:52.310
in the inner cities
but what he spoke about

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:55.070
was the soft bigotry
of low expectations.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:57.854
And that really applies
for our disabled community.

00:01:00.912 --> 00:01:01.870
We've looked at gender.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:02.745
We've looked at BAME.

00:01:02.745 --> 00:01:05.860
We're now looking at disability.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:08.080
Could you imagine having
500 Sheryl Sandbergs,

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.060
leaning in for disability
and with disability?

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.207
We would get this done.

00:01:11.207 --> 00:01:13.540
If we want disability to be
meaningfully at the business

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:14.860
table we need the leaders.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:15.985
It's got to be the leaders.

00:01:15.985 --> 00:01:18.100
The uncomfortable truth
is, they don't exist.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:21.490
I believe, and many do, that
the diversity and inclusion

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:23.700
agenda is very
difficult for business,

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.560
where we're pitting
humanity against each other.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.290
This year, we'll do gender.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:29.095
We'll do race.

00:01:29.095 --> 00:01:32.050
We'll ..do Next
year, we'll do LGBTQ.

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.330
What are we talking about?

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:38.050
A la carte and
pick-and-mix inclusion?

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:39.160
Are you kidding me?

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:42.490
Since when did we think it
was OK to have a hierarchy

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.298
of exclusion or inclusion?

00:01:50.725 --> 00:01:51.880
My name's Erin Boyce.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:54.820
I work at Alliance Learning and
I'm a business administrator,

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.120
currently an apprentice.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:57.170
I'm registered blind.

00:01:57.170 --> 00:01:59.483
I have a condition called
retinitis pigmentosa.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:06.140
Actually, I moved out of my
parents' house about a week

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:07.130
after I left college.

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:10.520
I really felt determined
to get out and start living

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:12.620
this new chapter of my life.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.620
So that was in the July
that I left and I moved out.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:17.060
I thought my
prospects were pretty

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:19.730
positive about getting a job.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:21.470
And then I was
applying to things

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:25.460
and I wasn't getting
anything at all.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:28.160
And at this point I was still
putting my visual impairment

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:29.630
on my CV.

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:31.588
I felt like it was all
framed in a positive way

00:02:31.588 --> 00:02:33.047
and it shouldn't
have put them off.

00:02:33.047 --> 00:02:35.300
But I didn't get any
kind of response at all.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.730
Then in October I decided
to take that off my CV

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.360
and I got two
interviews that month.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.170
And I didn't put it on again.

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:42.470
I went to 18 interviews.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:45.260
And for most people
that's the give-up point.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.782
And I didn't because I
really still wanted to work,

00:02:48.782 --> 00:02:49.740
so I kept going for it.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:52.380
But I know a lot of
people who have given up,

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.770
and those are people that
are perfectly able to work.

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:56.990
And if you reached
out to them and you

00:02:56.990 --> 00:03:00.050
said, hey, we know this
employer is accepting,

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:02.150
we know that they are
willing to make adaptations,

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:03.733
maybe you should
apply to them, you're

00:03:03.733 --> 00:03:06.410
going to get high-quality
applicants because, actually, I

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:10.100
know in the case of visually
impaired people in general,

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:11.870
there is actually a
higher rate of them

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:14.450
going to university
than the general public

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:17.060
because they know that their
chances of getting a job

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:17.760
are much worse.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:19.790
So they go into higher
education more often.

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:25.450
I really do think it is
a massive opportunity

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:26.760
for employers.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:30.070
And it's something,
being on the inside now,

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:33.280
having this opportunity to
hopefully make a difference

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.020
if I can, having had
my personal experience.

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:39.945
I'm trying now to actually get
us to tap into that because I

00:03:39.945 --> 00:03:42.070
think there are just...
there's this massive amount

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:44.740
of high-quality
applicants out there.

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:46.078
And they're more resilient.

00:03:46.078 --> 00:03:46.870
They're more loyal.

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:48.640
When you've slogged through
all these interviews

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.432
and you've had all
these things said to you

00:03:50.432 --> 00:03:53.500
and you feel like utter garbage,
and then somebody treats you

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:55.043
like you're not
a burden for once

00:03:55.043 --> 00:03:57.460
and they're willing to make
all these adaptations for you,

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.110
you know what you've
got at that point.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:04.600
So Alan, here we
have the statistics

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:08.020
relating to disability
and employment in Britain?

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:08.605
That's right.

00:04:08.605 --> 00:04:11.620
Probably bleaker than any of
the other data we've looked at.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.120
Well, it depends
how you look at it.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:14.710
I mean, one thing
I could say is we

00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:16.630
could start with a
good-news story, June,

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:18.940
which is that if you look
at from when the figures

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:21.760
that we've got that we can
go back as far as 2013,

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:23.950
there's a consistent
trend in the employment

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:25.772
rate of disabled people.

00:04:25.772 --> 00:04:27.205
Consistent increase, yeah.

00:04:27.205 --> 00:04:28.420
Both for men and for women.

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:32.080
And in fact, the employment
rate of women with disability,

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.000
you can see it's actually
accelerated a little bit faster

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.830
than the employment rate
for men with disability

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:41.530
and crucially has crossed
this 50 per cent line

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:42.690
for the first time.

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:46.180
So the employment rate
overall for both men and women

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:48.640
is now over 50 per cent.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:50.110
But just over 50 per cent.

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:52.120
Just over 50 per cent, and so...

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:55.040
And we would not be celebrating
that for any other group.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:55.540
Exactly.

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:58.930
So in fact, that's exactly
where this term, the disability

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:02.380
employment gap, pops up
because that line now

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:03.820
looks slightly less impressive.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:07.300
That's the same data we've just
been looking at, starting at 0

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:08.470
and finishing at 100.

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:10.367
So this is the entire
scale of the chart,

00:05:10.367 --> 00:05:12.450
and you can see this is a
very modest improvement.

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:15.520
But I mean, the real putting
those numbers into context

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:19.690
really only happens when you put
the employment rate for people

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:21.620
without disability on top.

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:22.120
OK?

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:24.560
And so you can see that
there really is this...

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:25.060
this thing.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:28.272
And this is what we're calling
the disability employment gap.

00:05:28.272 --> 00:05:29.980
And if you look at
how those figures have

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:34.450
changed since 2013, there's been
a modest narrowing of the gap.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:37.900
It was 33.1 percentage
points back in 2013.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:39.486
It's now down to 28.9 per cent.

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:48.150
My name is
Gemma-Louise Stevenson,

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:51.380
and I am a freelance
reporter for Sky Sports.

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:58.060
Alongside my reporting,
I'm also an athlete.

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:00.070
I don't like to stop.

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:01.840
I'm quite busy.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:03.970
I just want to live
life to the full,

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:06.946
and I want to make
the most of life.

00:06:06.946 --> 00:06:07.910
Oh, my god.

00:06:09.597 --> 00:06:10.320
Yeah.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:14.870
So Gemma, here we are at
Sky, your place of work...

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:17.800
my place of work
sometimes too, actually.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:21.320
I'm going to ask you what sounds
like a very dumb question.

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:25.790
But for employers, how do they
advertise to disabled people?

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:27.065
So what is that?

00:06:27.065 --> 00:06:30.710
What is the thing that they
need to do to make it very clear

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:32.168
that this is who
they're targeting?

00:06:32.168 --> 00:06:34.835
I mean, I think for me, it's all
very well having these training

00:06:34.835 --> 00:06:35.360
schemes.

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:38.960
Like, I went through a
training scheme myself.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:40.730
However, it was a very
negative experience

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:44.990
because the whole atmosphere
wasn't inclusive originally

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:45.705
to start with.

00:06:45.705 --> 00:06:46.205
OK

00:06:46.205 --> 00:06:48.538
I they're a good idea, but
I'm also very cynical of them

00:06:48.538 --> 00:06:52.252
because you can have all these
great, inclusive training

00:06:52.252 --> 00:06:53.960
schemes to get people
into the workplace,

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:55.250
to show them what it's
like, to give them

00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:57.125
experience so that they
can then go in a job.

00:06:57.125 --> 00:06:59.630
But if the workplace
to start with

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:01.940
is not an inclusive
workplace, you're

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:04.130
not going to retain those staff.

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:06.920
One thing I find about the Sky
is, they're very inclusive.

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:08.240
They treat me as an individual.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:10.593
I'm seen as a reporter
first before a wheelchair

00:07:10.593 --> 00:07:12.260
user who happens to
be a reporter, which

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:13.730
is so important.

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:16.160
Whether you're a
reporter like myself

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:19.160
or whether you're working
in HR or in administration,

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:21.950
you are working in HR, you
are working in administration,

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:24.530
you're working in media,
before your disability.

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:26.430
I use the social
model of disability,

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:29.790
which is the favoured version
amongst the disabled community.

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:32.780
So it's not saying that my
illness makes me disabled.

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:35.200
It's the environment
around me not being

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:36.905
accessible makes me disabled.

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:45.490
What's your message to
employers, and particularly HR

00:07:45.490 --> 00:07:49.240
directors, in terms of what they
can do when they are creating

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:52.810
an application and
also making sure

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:56.440
that that application reaches
people with disabilities,

00:07:56.440 --> 00:08:00.220
and then secondly, once they do
hire people with disabilities,

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:03.460
make sure that they're able to
thrive within their companies?

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:06.190
I think my biggest point
would be, treat everybody

00:08:06.190 --> 00:08:07.345
as an individual.

00:08:07.345 --> 00:08:07.945
Ah!

00:08:07.945 --> 00:08:08.545
One thing...

00:08:08.545 --> 00:08:09.550
Oh, no, no, no, no.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:10.955
This is powerful.

00:08:10.955 --> 00:08:11.455
Yeah.

00:08:11.455 --> 00:08:15.340
It's not about treating
everybody equally as such.

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:17.260
It's treating everybody
as an individual,

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:20.360
understanding that we all
have individual needs.

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:22.950
Yeah, because we all have
different needs to be equal.

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:25.930
To achieve equality is really
based on treating everybody

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:26.780
as an individual.

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:27.505
Yeah.

00:08:27.505 --> 00:08:30.520
It's very much talk to
the person because we

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:32.603
as disabled people are
very good at communicating.

00:08:32.603 --> 00:08:33.562
Because you have to be.

00:08:33.562 --> 00:08:34.065
We have to.

00:08:34.065 --> 00:08:34.469
Yeah.

00:08:34.469 --> 00:08:35.344
And do you know what?

00:08:35.344 --> 00:08:37.990
We know it's not
going to be perfect.

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:39.450
We know it's never...

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:41.140
we're not striving
for perfection.

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:44.596
We're just striving for a way
to work that enables us to work.

00:08:44.596 --> 00:08:45.572
Striving for inclusion.

00:08:45.572 --> 00:08:45.857
Exactly.

00:08:45.857 --> 00:08:46.450
Yeah.

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:48.100
And things will go wrong.

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:49.840
Even in the most
inclusive environments,

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:51.310
things will go wrong.

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:54.160
But do you know what makes the
difference between an employer

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:55.720
I want to stay with
and an employer

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:57.220
I want to leave behind?

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:00.015
The difference is is
that they listen to me.

00:09:00.015 --> 00:09:02.590
Hi, my name is Caroline
Casey, and I'm the founder

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:03.790
of The Valuable 500.

00:09:09.472 --> 00:09:12.400
In the BAME episode, we
talked about the dangers

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:13.580
of blanket terms.

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:14.080
Yeah.

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:16.630
When you lump all people
of colour together when

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:18.860
their lived experiences
are so different.

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:21.335
The same applies to
disability, whether...

00:09:21.335 --> 00:09:21.960
Absolutely.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:24.490
...whether it's physical,
sensory, or cognitive.

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:26.196
It's a very different
lived experience.

00:09:26.196 --> 00:09:27.568
And then acquired or congenital.

00:09:27.568 --> 00:09:28.110
There you go.

00:09:32.085 --> 00:09:34.770
You've just talked about
BAME or the soft bigotry.

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:36.900
Look what we're finding out.

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:38.370
This conversation
about disability

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:40.080
is no different, right?

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:41.325
This is not rocket science.

00:09:41.325 --> 00:09:44.430
But what I find very interesting
to your question about why

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:49.170
is the issue of disability
so on the edges of business

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:51.690
is because of the fear
of the complexity,

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:53.070
the fear of getting it wrong.

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:55.170
And actually, I'll
be really honest.

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:57.420
They would rather try
and deal with things

00:09:57.420 --> 00:09:59.828
that may be simpler and more
straightforward and easy

00:09:59.828 --> 00:10:01.620
because can you imagine
what a business has

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:04.320
to deal with in trying
to get it all right?

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:06.800
And I've noticed with
disability, they say,

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:08.687
but what do we do, Caroline?

00:10:08.687 --> 00:10:09.270
What do we do?

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:10.680
And I'm like, but
what did you do

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:12.540
when you started to
have the conversation

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:13.500
about the environment?

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:15.167
What did you do when
you started to have

00:10:15.167 --> 00:10:16.470
the conversation about gender?

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:19.110
Surely, you looked at what's
happening in your business.

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:20.550
And what's happening
elsewhere that's working.

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:21.870
What did your competitors do?

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:23.280
What did people outside do?

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:24.618
Did you go to experts?

00:10:24.618 --> 00:10:26.160
Did you talk to the
cohort of people?

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:28.650
Because the most important
thing about disability

00:10:28.650 --> 00:10:29.695
is just to ask.

00:10:29.695 --> 00:10:30.555
Yeah, just to ask.

00:10:30.555 --> 00:10:31.890
I don't know what your
lived experience is.

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:33.180
You don't know what mine is.

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:34.970
But we can have
the conversation.

00:10:39.485 --> 00:10:42.290
When you look at the employment
rates for different types

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:45.050
of disability, you realise that
looking at averages is very

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:49.840
dangerous because, actually, for
some disabilities like hearing,

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:52.177
the employment rate's
well over 60 per cent.

00:10:52.177 --> 00:10:52.885
Yes.

00:10:52.885 --> 00:10:55.190
But actually, down
here we've got things

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:57.767
like epilepsy, mental
illness, speech impediments.

00:10:57.767 --> 00:10:59.075
Learning difficulties.

00:10:59.075 --> 00:11:00.152
Yeah, exactly.

00:11:00.152 --> 00:11:01.860
And those rates are
very, very different.

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:03.890
So in fact, when you
look at the average

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:06.650
that we were just looking
at, which is just over half,

00:11:06.650 --> 00:11:09.723
51 per cent, you can see there's
the disability employment

00:11:09.723 --> 00:11:10.890
gap that we were looking at.

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:12.920
But look at these people
here with these kinds

00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:13.730
of disabilities.

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:15.065
The gap is much wider.

00:11:15.065 --> 00:11:15.763
Nowhere near.

00:11:15.763 --> 00:11:16.073
Nowhere near.

00:11:16.073 --> 00:11:17.510
They're nowhere near even
the average of people

00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:19.092
with disabilities in general.

00:11:19.092 --> 00:11:19.880
Exactly.

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:22.455
So with something
like epilepsy only,

00:11:22.455 --> 00:11:24.455
the employment rate is
around about 33 per cent.

00:11:24.455 --> 00:11:24.955
But is...

00:11:24.955 --> 00:11:27.813
I would've thought there are
certain forms of disability

00:11:27.813 --> 00:11:29.480
because the thing
with this is obviously

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:31.900
people are self-identifying.

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:34.490
There are a lot of people who
cover their disabilities, one

00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:37.460
of the things that Caroline
Casey was talking about.

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:39.410
This is something
she did herself.

00:11:39.410 --> 00:11:40.952
I would have thought
there were a lot

00:11:40.952 --> 00:11:43.580
of these forms of disabilities
that people probably

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:45.570
don't actually report.

00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:46.070
Yeah.

00:11:46.070 --> 00:11:47.600
I mean, that's one
of the weaknesses

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:49.910
of a self-reported
survey, which is

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:52.010
that you're entirely reliant
on someone submitting

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:52.790
that information.

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:54.957
And I mean, I think even
with physical disabilities,

00:11:54.957 --> 00:11:56.540
looking at this data,
the thing that's

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:58.670
really interesting is not
one of these categories

00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:02.420
actually reaches the
employment rate of people

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:03.850
without disabilities anyway.

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:06.350
Of course, the other thing that
we can talk about with this,

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:08.672
is that what we're seeing
here is our best data

00:12:08.672 --> 00:12:10.130
that we have on
this at the moment,

00:12:10.130 --> 00:12:12.672
but it's nowhere near like as
comprehensive as the data we

00:12:12.672 --> 00:12:14.630
would get for something
like the gender pay gap

00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:16.255
where we're asking
companies to report.

00:12:16.255 --> 00:12:19.162
This is... this is a
quarterly survey...

00:12:19.162 --> 00:12:21.620
Do you think that's the sort
of thing that needs to happen?

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:23.600
Do you think that we
need reporting on this?

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:25.670
Obviously, there's
discussions around reporting

00:12:25.670 --> 00:12:27.830
on BAME and pay gaps.

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:29.840
I think that certainly
the same arguments

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:32.510
that you could make historically
until we got the gender

00:12:32.510 --> 00:12:35.210
reporting and now with
the pressure for BAME,

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:39.595
I think you can make exactly the
same arguments for disability,

00:12:39.595 --> 00:12:41.720
partly because what we've
been looking at up to now

00:12:41.720 --> 00:12:44.100
is just the employment rates
and this general pattern.

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:46.940
The physical disabilities have
slightly higher employment

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:48.440
rates generally than mental.

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:51.860
But in fact, the Equalities
and Human Rights Commission

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:55.152
have carried out some analysis
of the disability pay gap.

00:12:55.152 --> 00:12:57.110
And that takes us much
more into this territory

00:12:57.110 --> 00:12:58.652
where we're
thinking, well, where

00:12:58.652 --> 00:13:00.860
do we start talking about
a requirement for companies

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:01.360
to report?

00:13:04.335 --> 00:13:07.145
Can we talk about the role
legislation has to play?

00:13:07.145 --> 00:13:07.645
So...

00:13:07.645 --> 00:13:08.185
Very important.

00:13:08.185 --> 00:13:10.143
I'm very interested in
some of the stuff that's

00:13:10.143 --> 00:13:13.030
going on in China in terms
of actually mandating

00:13:13.030 --> 00:13:15.400
large businesses to make
sure that a percentage

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:18.470
of the workforce is from
the disabled community.

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:20.700
What are your thoughts on
that kind of legislation?

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:21.200
OK.

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:23.390
So the quota question comes up.

00:13:23.390 --> 00:13:25.990
What I'm worried about
on the quota systems,

00:13:25.990 --> 00:13:29.680
in many of the OECD countries
who have these quotas about

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:33.370
employment figures, which
are often around 10 per cent

00:13:33.370 --> 00:13:35.350
representation in the
employment body...

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:36.983
And fines if you
don't meet them.

00:13:36.983 --> 00:13:38.400
50 per cent of
them pay the fines.

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:38.923
I know.

00:13:38.923 --> 00:13:40.113
They write them...

00:13:40.113 --> 00:13:40.905
Into their budgets.

00:13:40.905 --> 00:13:41.822
...into their budgets.

00:13:41.822 --> 00:13:43.060
So I'm just like, ugh.

00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:45.460
So then there's no point
to having legislation

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:50.280
if we are not going to
stand over our legislation.

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:50.780
Yeah.

00:13:54.175 --> 00:13:57.035
I'm Yana Kakar, the global
managing partner of Dalberg.

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:09.820
It was wonderful to have your
team observe the roundtable

00:14:09.820 --> 00:14:11.500
and sort of take
away the nuggets

00:14:11.500 --> 00:14:18.140
and then compile a to-do, an
action point, for CEOs and HRs.

00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:21.730
Often there is a
real focus on, am I

00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:24.040
recruiting a sufficiently
diverse group,

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:28.450
or am I incentivising
them sufficiently?

00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:34.510
But understanding how diversity
drives better team performance

00:14:34.510 --> 00:14:37.660
and then how to incentivise
diversity across the lifecycle

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:40.420
once you have a diverse
set of employees,

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:43.880
that's where the trick is.

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:46.410
If you think about what's the
value, what's the performance

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:49.840
value that we're talking about
from diversity and inclusion,

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:52.140
it's the diversity
of the thought

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:57.220
and the decision making and
the risk aversion and stopping

00:14:57.220 --> 00:14:57.720
groupthink.

00:14:57.720 --> 00:14:59.160
And just on that,
I mean, we still

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:02.640
have on those
dimensions of diversity

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:05.443
pretty poor measurement
and metrics,

00:15:05.443 --> 00:15:07.110
beyond the identifiable
characteristics.

00:15:07.110 --> 00:15:09.735
And it's good that we're doing
a better job of recording those.

00:15:09.735 --> 00:15:12.450
When it comes to
personality differences,

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:15.450
cognitive differences,
we're still at the foothills

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:18.960
there of thinking about how
to get a fix on that when

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:23.220
building genuinely diverse
teams across all its dimensions.

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:28.050
The composition of the balance
of characters in the team

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:29.480
is so critical.

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:29.980
Exactly.

00:15:29.980 --> 00:15:32.850
And the notion, as
Helena was saying,

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:37.410
of genuinely
team-based recruitment,

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:39.302
performance evaluation,
and promotion,

00:15:39.302 --> 00:15:41.260
that's... we're still a
world away from having.

00:15:49.690 --> 00:15:51.740
Ignition is a brewery
in southeast London.

00:15:51.740 --> 00:15:53.180
We have a taproom
as well, and we

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:55.680
sell draft ale and bottled ale.

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:57.860
Our secret is that our
staff team have learning

00:15:57.860 --> 00:16:00.485
disabilities, but the beer they
make is so good that we're able

00:16:00.485 --> 00:16:02.870
to sell it and pay them the
London living wage of £10.55

00:16:02.870 --> 00:16:03.510
an hour.

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:04.010
Wow.

00:16:04.010 --> 00:16:06.977
I mean, we've been looking
at the data in relation

00:16:06.977 --> 00:16:08.810
to people with disabilities
in the workplace

00:16:08.810 --> 00:16:11.120
and particularly
learning disabilities.

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:13.610
That group in particular
earn the least

00:16:13.610 --> 00:16:15.560
and are the least
likely to be employed.

00:16:15.560 --> 00:16:20.260
Is that why you decided to focus
on that side of disability?

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:20.760
Sort of.

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:22.135
So yeah, you're
absolutely right.

00:16:22.135 --> 00:16:25.237
It was 94 per cent
of people had no job.

00:16:25.237 --> 00:16:26.820
They've actually
stopped measuring it,

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:27.990
so I think the government
now have just given up.

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:28.823
Because it's so bad.

00:16:28.823 --> 00:16:29.990
What's the point?

00:16:29.990 --> 00:16:31.430
You're trying to sell an idea.

00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:32.420
And I think what we've
learned about this

00:16:32.420 --> 00:16:33.337
is, it's about people.

00:16:33.337 --> 00:16:34.340
People employ people.

00:16:34.340 --> 00:16:36.800
And also to create
the kind of culture

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:39.980
where your employees can
thrive because if you're

00:16:39.980 --> 00:16:42.020
taking somebody with
learning disabilities

00:16:42.020 --> 00:16:44.450
into an environment that
isn't prepared for them,

00:16:44.450 --> 00:16:47.340
sometimes you can be doing
that employee a disservice.

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:48.920
So what's wonderful
about here is,

00:16:48.920 --> 00:16:50.930
you've created the
kind of environment

00:16:50.930 --> 00:16:53.667
that's inclusive enough for
all your employees to thrive.

00:16:53.667 --> 00:16:54.250
Oh, thank you.

00:16:54.250 --> 00:16:54.925
And I think...

00:16:54.925 --> 00:16:56.120
It matters.

00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:57.230
I think what's good
about this is, actually,

00:16:57.230 --> 00:16:59.360
a lot of the changes that we've
made in comparison to places

00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:01.027
where I've worked
before, actually we've

00:17:01.027 --> 00:17:02.360
got a much healthier culture.

00:17:02.360 --> 00:17:03.860
And I've now gone
and employed those

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:05.329
in places where I will work.

00:17:05.329 --> 00:17:08.930
And it's a lot of the things
we've had to do about being

00:17:08.930 --> 00:17:11.710
patient or getting a good
routine or having a healthy...

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:14.210
the way in which you talk to
each other being really healthy

00:17:14.210 --> 00:17:15.690
are actually just
things anyone should do.

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:17.565
And I think we are an
extreme version of what

00:17:17.565 --> 00:17:20.270
I'd like the world to be
because our team is obviously

00:17:20.270 --> 00:17:21.510
very special.

00:17:21.510 --> 00:17:23.060
But I think the
impact that someone

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:25.460
could have with a learning
disability for the better

00:17:25.460 --> 00:17:28.369
in your business is really good
because it's great for morale.

00:17:28.369 --> 00:17:29.990
It's also great for
sort of, I think,

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:32.480
getting people just to
behave better and think,

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:36.305
actually, I'm at work, and I
need to show a good example,

00:17:36.305 --> 00:17:38.015
and I need to be
a better person.

00:17:38.015 --> 00:17:39.264
You're an economist by trade.

00:17:39.264 --> 00:17:39.764
Yes.

00:17:39.764 --> 00:17:42.130
We won't hold that against you.

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:45.500
But do you think that also
influenced your decision

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:48.230
to actually do something
about this problem,

00:17:48.230 --> 00:17:50.750
knowing how many
people were out of work

00:17:50.750 --> 00:17:53.690
and how much we're losing
out on as an economy

00:17:53.690 --> 00:17:54.523
as a result of that?

00:17:54.523 --> 00:17:55.023
Yeah.

00:17:55.023 --> 00:17:57.450
So it was a kind of... it's
a financial gamble in a way,

00:17:57.450 --> 00:17:57.950
I thought.

00:17:57.950 --> 00:18:00.735
The best way to show that our
guys can generate you profit

00:18:00.735 --> 00:18:01.985
an income is set up a company.

00:18:01.985 --> 00:18:02.420
Is to show it.

00:18:02.420 --> 00:18:02.735
Yeah.

00:18:02.735 --> 00:18:03.235
Yeah.

00:18:03.235 --> 00:18:04.850
And so we've taken
people with no...

00:18:04.850 --> 00:18:06.058
I have no brewing experience.

00:18:06.058 --> 00:18:07.512
They have no brewing experience.

00:18:07.512 --> 00:18:08.470
I'm not mad about beer.

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:10.040
I quite like our beer.

00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:13.240
And actually, we've created a
business that is surplus making

00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:16.035
and is a good employer
based on their talents.

00:18:22.120 --> 00:18:26.435
For anybody wanting to replicate
your model, for corporations

00:18:26.435 --> 00:18:28.810
who want to look at doing
something like this on a larger

00:18:28.810 --> 00:18:31.020
scale, what are the pitfalls?

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:32.260
What are the difficulties?

00:18:32.260 --> 00:18:34.750
Because let's be
frank about that too.

00:18:34.750 --> 00:18:36.550
So I think it's a
big cultural change.

00:18:36.550 --> 00:18:38.640
And we all know cultural
change is the hardest.

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:40.857
Yeah, it's really
difficult. And there

00:18:40.857 --> 00:18:42.940
needs to be buy-in at the
top, is the first thing.

00:18:42.940 --> 00:18:45.605
So the top honcho,
they need to say

00:18:45.605 --> 00:18:47.230
I'm really committed
to this, and we're

00:18:47.230 --> 00:18:47.900
going to make it happen.

00:18:47.900 --> 00:18:49.420
And without that,
it won't happen.

00:18:49.420 --> 00:18:50.170
It's very simple.

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:51.628
And then I think
there's been a lot

00:18:51.628 --> 00:18:53.830
of the kind of
systematisation of employment

00:18:53.830 --> 00:18:55.038
for people with disabilities.

00:18:55.038 --> 00:18:57.560
So there's a checklist, and
they make them interview-ready.

00:18:57.560 --> 00:18:59.290
Then there's a CV-ready thing.

00:18:59.290 --> 00:19:01.400
And we just largely
discarded that too.

00:19:01.400 --> 00:19:01.900
You did?

00:19:01.900 --> 00:19:02.451
Yes, we did.

00:19:02.451 --> 00:19:02.951
We did.

00:19:02.951 --> 00:19:04.770
So you got rid of
that whole process.

00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:06.687
So what we've done is,
we've started very much

00:19:06.687 --> 00:19:09.580
with the people and said,
do you want to work with us?

00:19:09.580 --> 00:19:11.080
Are you really keen?

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:11.627
Yeah.

00:19:11.627 --> 00:19:12.710
And then they'll say, yes.

00:19:12.710 --> 00:19:13.210
OK.

00:19:13.210 --> 00:19:15.827
Well, come and just do a shift
because, actually, that's

00:19:15.827 --> 00:19:18.160
the best way for us to learn,
because even if they can't

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:20.290
do any of it, that's fine.

00:19:20.290 --> 00:19:22.330
But you can tell if
the aptitude is there.

00:19:22.330 --> 00:19:25.182
And if they sort of go, OK...
because pulling a pint is

00:19:25.182 --> 00:19:27.140
actually really... well,
I find it really hard.

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:27.655
And so...

00:19:27.655 --> 00:19:27.925
It is.

00:19:27.925 --> 00:19:28.801
I've never done it.

00:19:28.801 --> 00:19:29.830
It's really difficult
because it come...

00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:30.460
the head's too big.

00:19:30.460 --> 00:19:31.200
It's too small.

00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:31.510
It's too flat.

00:19:31.510 --> 00:19:32.093
It's too cold.

00:19:32.093 --> 00:19:34.177
But you can tell if
someone's going to keep

00:19:34.177 --> 00:19:35.260
on going till they get it.

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:37.000
And then once they
get it, it's...

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:37.625
They've got it.

00:19:42.170 --> 00:19:45.810
If you want to truly, I suppose,
maximise the performance

00:19:45.810 --> 00:19:49.020
potential of your diverse
teams, you bring them on board,

00:19:49.020 --> 00:19:52.140
and then you identify where
are there sort of hidden

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:54.060
constraints to top performance?

00:19:54.060 --> 00:19:56.550
Where are there ways in which
diversity, which we know

00:19:56.550 --> 00:19:59.580
has been statistically
proven to be a key driver

00:19:59.580 --> 00:20:00.630
of financial performance?

00:20:00.630 --> 00:20:05.100
I mean, it was shocking to
me to see that it was so...

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:07.650
so well quantified
that literally it's

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:11.700
a percentage of increased
financial performance

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:13.380
if you have a diverse team.

00:20:13.380 --> 00:20:17.218
And you can just see the
needle go up and down.

00:20:17.218 --> 00:20:18.593
But yet there's
still resistance.

00:20:18.593 --> 00:20:19.093
There is.

00:20:19.093 --> 00:20:20.642
It makes no sense.

00:20:20.642 --> 00:20:21.210
I know.

00:20:21.210 --> 00:20:22.380
Well, it's...

00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:23.060
you sort of...

00:20:23.060 --> 00:20:28.380
we spend enough time, I
think, on the diagnosis

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:30.610
or the demonstration
of the business case.

00:20:30.610 --> 00:20:31.115
OK.

00:20:31.115 --> 00:20:34.470
But then committing and
executing the action...

00:20:34.470 --> 00:20:35.760
that's what the next step is.

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:36.260
Yeah.

00:20:36.260 --> 00:20:36.760
OK.

00:20:36.760 --> 00:20:38.340
So we have workforce.

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:42.690
Let's talk about customers
and consumers in terms of what

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:47.100
a CEO or a leader within an
organisation can do to add

00:20:47.100 --> 00:20:49.405
value in relation to
diversity for the customers.

00:20:49.405 --> 00:20:51.930
The interesting thing about
customers, or consumers,

00:20:51.930 --> 00:20:57.540
is, it is only a medium-known
fact the degree to which

00:20:57.540 --> 00:21:02.370
the diverse consumer is growing
in strength, buying power

00:21:02.370 --> 00:21:05.130
strength, decision-making
power strength.

00:21:05.130 --> 00:21:06.360
I found it fascinating.

00:21:06.360 --> 00:21:10.440
One of your attendees at the
CEO roundtable, Karen Blackett,

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:14.700
CEO - powerful CEO in her own
right - and she said, look,

00:21:14.700 --> 00:21:18.900
over the last decade, we have
seen minority buying power go

00:21:18.900 --> 00:21:22.050
from £30bn-odd to £300bn.

00:21:22.050 --> 00:21:22.860
So let's just...

00:21:22.860 --> 00:21:24.795
let's pause and
think about that.

00:21:24.795 --> 00:21:25.410
Yeah.

00:21:25.410 --> 00:21:30.660
So shifting gears to say, well,
who is this diverse customer

00:21:30.660 --> 00:21:32.475
that I have out there,
and understanding...

00:21:32.475 --> 00:21:34.177
That I'm not servicing properly.

00:21:34.177 --> 00:21:36.870
Exactly, and understanding,
how do I actually

00:21:36.870 --> 00:21:40.200
tailor myself, my product, my
solution, my approach, in order

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:41.210
to capture them?

00:21:46.297 --> 00:21:51.210
The most provocative way to get
anybody in business interested

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:52.545
is to look at their bottom line.

00:21:52.545 --> 00:21:52.775
Yeah.

00:21:52.775 --> 00:21:54.355
So let's just talk about the UK.

00:21:54.355 --> 00:21:54.855
OK.

00:21:54.855 --> 00:21:56.250
You're worth £249bn.

00:21:56.250 --> 00:21:56.760
billion.

00:21:56.760 --> 00:21:59.660
That is what this disability
community is worth.

00:21:59.660 --> 00:22:02.490
There's massive competition
going on on those high streets.

00:22:02.490 --> 00:22:03.660
Why are you not listening?

00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:05.160
I think it's really
interesting when

00:22:05.160 --> 00:22:08.730
we see that supermarkets
supplying for the 400,000

00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:12.270
vegans because they
see it as value, right?

00:22:12.270 --> 00:22:15.060
But let's be honest, because
they see it as value.

00:22:15.060 --> 00:22:15.746
Well, what about

00:22:15.746 --> 00:22:17.163
Twenty per cent
of the population?

00:22:17.163 --> 00:22:18.630
What about 20 per cent
of the population here?

00:22:18.630 --> 00:22:21.090
So I think Barclays have
always made the intention very

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:24.750
clear they want to be the most
accessible and inclusive FTSE

00:22:24.750 --> 00:22:25.860
company.

00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:28.950
And they do that through
their consumer offering.

00:22:28.950 --> 00:22:34.977
But the best example of all
time for any of this is Apple.

00:22:34.977 --> 00:22:36.265
Really?

00:22:36.265 --> 00:22:36.765
Why?

00:22:36.765 --> 00:22:37.400
How so?

00:22:37.400 --> 00:22:39.780
Because Steve Jobs, we know,
was probably very difficult,

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:40.840
but he was a visionary.

00:22:40.840 --> 00:22:44.280
And he wanted to create
the most beautiful products

00:22:44.280 --> 00:22:47.100
that everybody else in
the world could use.

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:50.168
That's called universal design.

00:22:50.168 --> 00:22:51.960
Apple was the very
first brand in the world

00:22:51.960 --> 00:22:54.940
to reach $1tn Why do
you think that is?

00:22:54.940 --> 00:22:59.250
It's because there are more of
us who can use their products.

00:22:59.250 --> 00:23:02.220
Actually, Apple is a brand that
most of the disabled community

00:23:02.220 --> 00:23:03.035
will choose.

00:23:03.035 --> 00:23:06.352
So was that a consideration
when they were designing...

00:23:06.352 --> 00:23:06.900
Yeah.

00:23:06.900 --> 00:23:08.107
He just wanted to have...

00:23:08.107 --> 00:23:08.940
Wow, their hardware.

00:23:08.940 --> 00:23:10.260
... beautiful products...

00:23:10.260 --> 00:23:11.257
I had no idea.

00:23:11.257 --> 00:23:12.690
...for everyone.

00:23:12.690 --> 00:23:15.510
Now, they don't shout
about being an inclusive

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:16.830
or an accessible company.

00:23:16.830 --> 00:23:18.580
It's universal design.

00:23:18.580 --> 00:23:20.370
Sometimes we forget
that if we get

00:23:20.370 --> 00:23:23.810
design right for the full
spectrum of disability,

00:23:23.810 --> 00:23:25.560
we'll probably get it
right for all of us.

00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:28.500
I kind of think the business
case to incentivise business,

00:23:28.500 --> 00:23:30.840
let me just tell you,
it's an $8tn market.

00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:33.010
It's a brand opportunity.

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:34.680
It's an uncluttered space.

00:23:34.680 --> 00:23:36.600
It's the acquisition
and retention of talent.

00:23:36.600 --> 00:23:37.690
It's about innovation.

00:23:37.690 --> 00:23:38.520
It's productivity.

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:42.630
The next generation really
care about spending money

00:23:42.630 --> 00:23:45.190
and working with companies that
allow you to be who you are.

00:23:45.190 --> 00:23:46.895
Why have we not seen
accelerated change?

00:23:46.895 --> 00:23:48.270
I'll go back to
where we began...

00:23:48.270 --> 00:23:50.220
because the most powerful
force on this planet

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:53.190
is making a decision to leave
the disability community

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:55.310
as an invisible market.

00:23:55.310 --> 00:23:57.750
And unless it gets
into this game,

00:23:57.750 --> 00:24:00.540
meaningfully invests
in it, we will

00:24:00.540 --> 00:24:02.637
continue to see, no
matter what, no matter

00:24:02.637 --> 00:24:05.220
what legislation you do in the
world, because let's be honest.

00:24:05.220 --> 00:24:08.070
Money runs the world -
power, money, business.

00:24:08.070 --> 00:24:09.240
It affects politics.

00:24:09.240 --> 00:24:11.940
And I don't think any
legislation will be able

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:14.700
to solve this if businesses
don't take responsibility,

00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:15.240
but...

00:24:15.240 --> 00:24:16.780
and take up the opportunity.

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:19.830
I believe diversity has
a triple-line benefit

00:24:19.830 --> 00:24:23.250
to business for innovation,
productivity, and bottom line.

00:24:23.250 --> 00:24:25.290
It's a triple-line
bonus, the individual,

00:24:25.290 --> 00:24:27.760
to society and peace at large,
and to the business bottom

00:24:27.760 --> 00:24:28.260
line.

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:31.540
I mean, what is not to like?

